### What is cycle detection in a linked list?

A cycle in a linked list occurs when a node's `next` pointer points back to a previous node in the chain, creating an infinite loop. Any naive traversal that follows `next` pointers until `null` will run forever if a cycle exists. Floyd's cycle detection algorithm, also called the tortoise and hare, determines whether a linked list contains a cycle using two pointers and no extra memory.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransactionNode {
    public String txnId;
    public BigDecimal amount;
    public TransactionNode next;

    public TransactionNode(String txnId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.txnId = txnId;
        this.amount = amount;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

### How does Floyd's algorithm work?

Start both `slow` and `fast` at the head. Loop while `fast != null && fast.next != null`. Each iteration, advance `slow` by one node and `fast` by two nodes. After each move, compare the two pointers. If `slow == fast`, a cycle exists. If the loop exits because `fast` became `null`, the list is acyclic.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;

public class CycleDetector {
    public static boolean hasCycle(TransactionNode head) {
        TransactionNode slow = head;
        TransactionNode fast = head;

        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
            if (slow == fast) {
                return true; // cycle detected
            }
        }
        return false; // no cycle
    }
}
```

### Why are the two pointers guaranteed to meet inside a cycle?

Once both pointers enter the cycle, the fast pointer gains exactly one node on the slow pointer every step. Think of it as the gap between them shrinking by one each iteration. If the gap is currently `d` nodes, after `d` more steps the gap is zero and they collide. They cannot jump past each other because the gap changes by exactly one per step.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CycleMeetingProof {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Build chain: TXN-001 -> TXN-002 -> TXN-003 -> TXN-004 -> back to TXN-002
        TransactionNode n1 = new TransactionNode("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        TransactionNode n2 = new TransactionNode("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("2000"));
        TransactionNode n3 = new TransactionNode("TXN-003", new BigDecimal("3000"));
        TransactionNode n4 = new TransactionNode("TXN-004", new BigDecimal("4000"));
        n1.next = n2; n2.next = n3; n3.next = n4; n4.next = n2; // cycle!

        TransactionNode slow = n1, fast = n1;
        int step = 0;
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
            step++;
            System.out.println("Step " + step + ": slow=" + slow.txnId + " fast=" + fast.txnId);
            if (slow == fast) {
                System.out.println("Met at " + slow.txnId + " after " + step + " steps");
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Step 1: slow=TXN-002 fast=TXN-003
Step 2: slow=TXN-003 fast=TXN-003
Met at TXN-003 after 2 steps
```

### How is this different from the hash-set approach?

The hash-set approach stores every visited node in a `HashSet` and checks for repeats. It also detects cycles in O(n) time but uses O(n) space. Floyd's algorithm achieves the same result in O(1) space.

| Feature | Floyd's Algorithm | Hash-Set Approach |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space | O(1) | O(n) |
| Finds cycle start | Needs second phase | Immediately (first repeat) |
| Code complexity | Simple two-pointer | Requires set operations |

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

// Hash-set approach: O(n) time, O(n) space
public static boolean hasCycleHashSet(TransactionNode head) {
    Set<TransactionNode> visited = new HashSet<>();
    TransactionNode current = head;
    while (current != null) {
        if (!visited.add(current)) {
            return true; // already visited this node
        }
        current = current.next;
    }
    return false;
}
```

### Why must the comparison be reference equality?

The comparison `slow == fast` checks whether both pointers reference the same node object, not whether the values are equal. Two different nodes can hold the same value, and comparing values would produce a false positive. Cycle detection is a structural check, not a value check.

```java
// WRONG: value comparison gives false positive on duplicate values
if (slow.amount.equals(fast.amount)) { ... } // not a cycle!

// CORRECT: reference comparison detects structural loop
if (slow == fast) { ... } // same node object
```

### What are the edge cases?

The main edge cases are an empty list, a single node with no cycle, and a single node that points to itself.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class CycleEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Empty list
        System.out.println("Empty: " + CycleDetector.hasCycle(null));

        // Single node, no cycle
        TransactionNode single = new TransactionNode("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("1000"));
        System.out.println("Single no cycle: " + CycleDetector.hasCycle(single));

        // Single node pointing to itself
        single.next = single;
        System.out.println("Single self-cycle: " + CycleDetector.hasCycle(single));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: false
Single no cycle: false
Single self-cycle: true
```

### What about the fast pointer guard condition?

The guard `fast != null && fast.next != null` is essential because the fast pointer reads two links ahead. If you only check `fast != null`, then `fast.next.next` could throw a `NullPointerException` when `fast.next` is null. The double guard ensures both steps are safe.

```java
// Guard explained:
// fast != null       -> fast itself exists
// fast.next != null  -> fast can safely do fast.next.next
while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
    slow = slow.next;        // one step
    fast = fast.next.next;   // two steps, safe because of guard
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Floyd's algorithm is the foundation for several related problems. The next step after detection is finding the cycle start node, which uses a second phase where one pointer resets to the head and both advance one step at a time until they meet at the entry point. The same tortoise-and-hare principle applies to detecting cycles in any sequence defined by a function, such as the "find the duplicate number" problem where array indices are treated as links.

In production at the National Bank of India, cycle detection prevents infinite traversal in transaction chains built from imported data. A corrupted import can create a loop, and the cycle detector runs as a validation step before processing begins.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;

public class ChainValidator {
    public static void validateChain(TransactionNode head) {
        if (CycleDetector.hasCycle(head)) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Corrupted transaction chain detected: cycle found. "
                + "Import rejected for manual review."
            );
        }
    }
}
```
